//? Title: Group comparisons with unequal variances and tied ranks
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

The three groups in this example have different sample sizes and spreads.
Welch's one-way ANOVA tests whether their population means are equal, using
the estimated precision of each group mean. For two groups its F statistic
equals the square of the Welch t statistic.

Games–Howell estimates each pairwise mean difference. The studentized-range
reference accounts for the number of groups in the family, while the degrees
of freedom for each pair come from its own two sample variances. Both methods
assume independent observations within and across normally distributed groups.

> Compare three groups

```{cpp, id=unequal-variance-groups, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/anova_test.hpp>
#include <print>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    namespace stats = datamunge::stats;
    const std::vector<double> values{1, 2, 4, 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 3, 4, 5, 5};
    const std::vector<std::size_t> sizes{3, 5, 4};
    const auto fit = stats::welch_anova(values, sizes);
    std::println("Welch F({:.0f}, {:.3f}) = {:.4f}, p = {:.5f}.\n",
                 fit.parameter1, fit.parameter2, fit.statistic, fit.p_value);
    std::println("| Comparison | Mean difference | Pair df | 95% interval | Adjusted p |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: |");
    for (const auto& row : stats::games_howell(values, sizes)) {
        std::println("| {} minus {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | [{:.3f}, {:.3f}] | {:.5f} |",
                     row.group1 + 1, row.group2 + 1, row.difference,
                     row.degrees_of_freedom, row.lower, row.upper, row.p_adjusted);
    }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Welch F(2, 4.775) = 4.1238, p = 0.09113.

| Comparison | Mean difference | Pair df | 95% interval | Adjusted p |
| --- | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: |
| 2 minus 1 | 6.667 | 5.192 | [-0.711, 14.044] | 0.07077 |
| 3 minus 1 | 1.917 | 3.169 | [-2.134, 5.967] | 0.27423 |
| 3 minus 2 | -4.750 | 4.403 | [-12.181, 2.681] | 0.17855 |
// result_end

Group 2 has the highest observed mean. The interval for group 2 minus
group 1 is wide because observations in group 2 vary substantially.
Each Games–Howell interval includes zero at the 95% family confidence level.

> Compare the group spreads

The same observations can be used to test a different hypothesis, equality
of group variances. Bartlett uses the normal-theory sampling distribution.
Levene and Brown–Forsythe compare absolute deviations from the group means
and medians, respectively. Fligner–Killeen uses ranks of the median-centered
deviations.

```{cpp, id=group-spreads, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/anova_test.hpp>
#include <print>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::stats;
    const std::vector<double> values{1, 2, 4, 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 3, 4, 5, 5};
    const std::vector<std::size_t> sizes{3, 5, 4};
    std::println("| Test | Statistic | p-value |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: |");
    const auto print_row = [](const HypothesisTestResult& fit) {
        std::println("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.5f} |", fit.method, fit.statistic, fit.p_value);
    };
    print_row(levene_test(values, sizes, false));
    print_row(levene_test(values, sizes));
    print_row(bartlett_test(values, sizes));
    print_row(fligner_killeen_test(values, sizes));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Test | Statistic | p-value |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Levene test of equal variances | 3.6136 | 0.07047 |
| Brown-Forsythe test of equal variances | 3.5501 | 0.07301 |
| Bartlett test of homogeneity of variances | 6.4958 | 0.03886 |
| Fligner-Killeen test of homogeneity of variances | 4.1283 | 0.12692 |
// result_end

> Compare pooled ranks with Dunn's test

Dunn's comparisons pool observations across the whole family before assigning
ranks. Ties receive their average rank. The example uses Holm adjustment over
all three pairs.

```{cpp, id=dunn-ranks, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/anova_test.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    const auto rows = datamunge::stats::dunn_test({0, 2, 2, 1, 2, 4, 5, 3, 3}, {3, 4, 2});
    std::println("| Comparison | Mean rank difference | z | p-value | Holm p-value |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const auto& row : rows)
        std::println("| {} minus {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |",
                     row.group1 + 1, row.group2 + 1, row.rank_difference,
                     row.statistic, row.p_value, row.p_adjusted);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Comparison | Mean rank difference | z | p-value | Holm p-value |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2 minus 1 | 2.750 | 1.343 | 0.1793 | 0.4581 |
| 3 minus 1 | 3.500 | 1.430 | 0.1527 | 0.4581 |
| 3 minus 2 | 0.750 | 0.323 | 0.7467 | 0.7467 |
// result_end

> References

- [Welch (1951), On the Comparison of Several Mean Values|https://doi.org/10.2307/2332579]
- [Games and Howell (1976), Pairwise Multiple Comparison Procedures with Unequal N's and/or Variances|https://doi.org/10.3102/10769986001002113]
- [Dunn (1964), Multiple Comparisons Using Rank Sums|https://doi.org/10.2307/1266041]
